\description{
Estimate rater severity when the raters are modeled as draws from a specified
population. Each rater has one effect shared across everyone they rate.
The rating-scale model (RSM) uses approximate marginal maximum likelihood,
with optional additive fixed facets. For a local effect within one person's
performance instead, see \code{\link[=fit_mfrm_testlet]{fit_mfrm_testlet()}}.

Evaluate a shared-rater RSM at explicitly supplied ability values.
}
\details{
Fits the crossed random-rater RSM
\deqn{\log\{P(Y_{prj}=k)/P(Y_{prj}=k-1)\} = \theta_p - u_r - x_{prj}'\beta - \delta_k,}
with independent \eqn{\theta_p \sim N(0, \sigma_p^2)} and shared rater
severities \eqn{u_r \sim N(0, \sigma_r^2)}, sum-to-zero fixed-facet effects
\eqn{\beta}, and free adjacent steps \eqn{\delta_k}. Person integrals use
Gauss-Hermite quadrature conditional on \eqn{u}, and the joint shared-rater
integral uses RTMB's Laplace approximation (unit weights, connected design,
observed categories). Extreme fixed-facet separation and zero-variance
boundaries (checked via one-sided variance scores) fail \code{checks$NumericalReady}
or withhold regular intervals.

Calibration and conditional-mode rater bounds (\code{ConditionalSD} vs
\code{PredictionSE}) are omitted by default and requested explicitly via
\code{confint(fit, parm = "calibration" | "rater_sd" | "raters")}; finite-sample
coverage is not guaranteed with few raters (see the
\href{https://ryuya-dot-com.github.io/mfrmr/dev/articles/mfrmr-random-raters.html}{online random-rater guide}
for likelihood and coverage benchmarks, and \code{\link[=fit_mfrm_testlet]{fit_mfrm_testlet()}} for
Person-specific local dependence).

Predictions condition on estimated calibration and supplied ability.
They do not propagate calibration-estimation uncertainty or uncertainty
about ability. Observed-rater integration uses the conditional Laplace
mean/mode and covariance, not the calibration-adjusted prediction SE.
New-rater integration uses the population SD: substituting severity zero
generally produces different probabilities.

Rows are marginal probabilities for individual future ratings, not a joint
distribution or an interval for their average. Ratings with the same new
rater ID share one random severity in the model; multiplying these row
probabilities would discard that dependence. This function does not score
latent abilities from responses or impute missing assigned scores.
Use \code{\link[=score_mfrm_random_rater]{score_mfrm_random_rater()}} for conditional Person scoring from a
complete joint response roster.
Supplying a score column in \code{newdata} does not update rater distributions
or estimate abilities. Use the scoring function and its complete-roster
rules when the input is a new batch of observed responses.

\code{plot()} method: Plot observed-rater severity under a shared-rater RSM

For \code{plot()}: Points are conditional modes of severity relative to the rater
population mean zero; positive values mean more severe ratings. No individual
rater intervals are supplied automatically, including for earlier saved
fits. Explicit normal whiskers are approximate 95\% prediction intervals,
not qualified rater classifications. At an estimated zero variance or with
unresolved numerical checks, even requested whiskers are unavailable.

For \code{parm = "rater_sd"}, fixed facets, steps and any estimated
ability SD are refitted at
each candidate rater SD, including zero. A specified known ability SD
stays fixed. Earlier saved fits retain their known N(0,1) population. The
search brackets a lower and upper crossing of twice the log-likelihood
loss with \code{qchisq(level, df = 1)}, around the fitted SD. It uses the same approximate marginal likelihood as
the fit. The lower bound is zero when the zero-variance submodel belongs
to the likelihood-ratio region. An estimated boundary can therefore have a positive upper
limit even though ordinary Wald intervals are unavailable.
Each nonzero endpoint must attain the likelihood-ratio cutoff to
\code{1e-4 * min(1, cutoff)} absolute accuracy. Insufficient floating-point
resolution stops the search instead of reporting coincident limits.
Profile refits require positive nuisance information. With known ability
SD, nuisance curvature is checked at both integration orders.
These local numerical checks do not prove a global optimum or exclude
disconnected likelihood-ratio regions between sampled candidates.

This is an asymptotic likelihood-ratio interval, not a finite-sample coverage
guarantee. The chi-square reference is nonregular at a true zero variance;
the usual one-degree-of-freedom cutoff is conservative under the standard
single-variance boundary asymptotics. Few raters, Laplace error and design
misspecification can alter coverage. The numerical checks do not establish
those asymptotic conditions. The calculation stops if the fitted or profiled
ability variance is an estimated zero boundary; that additional nuisance
boundary requires a different reference distribution for inference.
No ability-SD interval is supplied.
This interval does not quantify the predictive
distribution of a replacement rater, whose variation is a different target.

The separate \code{parm = "raters"} calculation uses the conditional mode plus
or minus \code{qnorm((1 + level)/2) * PredictionSE}. It targets realized,
uncentered rater effects relative to the population mean. First-order
calibration uncertainty does not ensure nominal coverage. In particular,
few-rater coverage remains unresolved; see
\href{https://ryuya-dot-com.github.io/mfrmr/dev/articles/mfrmr-random-raters.html}{online random-rater guide}. Requesting this approximation does not
make it qualified for classification or exclusion of raters. It is not a
profile interval, a bootstrap or an interval for a difference of raters.
}
